#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110382 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87c18ea8-af12-52bd-8420-ec24ed63de65" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "42313322-7535-5969-ad84-958449e48c1b" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3480" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $y$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\dfrac{5}{9}$. What is the $x$-coordinate of $A$?" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{5}{9}\n$$\n\ninto the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} x^2+y^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{5}{9}\\right)^2&=1 \\\\[5pt] x^2+\\left(\\dfrac{25}{81}\\right)&=1 \\\\[5pt] x^2&= 1-\\dfrac{25}{81} \\\\[5pt] x^2&= \\dfrac{56}{81} \\\\[5pt] x&= \\sqrt{\\dfrac{56}{81}} \\\\[5pt] x&= \\pm \\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $x$ must be positive. So, we select the positive answer.\n\nTherefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3482" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1453/Source/Images/a3304e0765fc9114069ac2c32af82d61.png)" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{2}{3})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{4}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant.\n\nSo our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "bbf71023-510c-54af-9d0c-4df6744e3007" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3484" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\csc\\theta$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1453/Source/Images/fbbb469f7aac22ff545c74f910fb8881.png)" :question/worked-solution "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{24}{49}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{5}{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So,\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}\n$$\n\nFinally, knowing that\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/id #uuid "ba6cc6a0-dbb8-58d2-9d02-c76310028d4e" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3485" :question/problem "The diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{4}{5}$, what is $\\tan\\theta$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1453/Source/Images/e45c2103a055d4e2fe29c84aa4497c7a.png)" :question/worked-solution "We're given that\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nat the point $P$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin^{2} θ + \\cos^{2} θ &= 1 \\\\\n\\sin^{2} θ &= 1 - \\cos^{2} θ \\\\\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\cos^{2} θ}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin θ &= ± \\sqrt{1 - (\\frac{4}{5})^{2}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{1 - \\frac{16}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\\n&= ± \\frac{3}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta}\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e11348d0-6f04-5538-8c61-81cb1bcfce75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107212} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd673ca-a545-5697-a3e3-8fcd04384a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107213} {:answer/id #uuid "2c5ee479-bd5c-516f-9781-5c72af5726c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107214} {:answer/id #uuid "6a031f8a-1b43-5f03-a34c-cc10eefef83e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107215} {:answer/id #uuid "6e223962-8898-546e-a8b6-d3577a292fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c5ee479-bd5c-516f-9781-5c72af5726c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107214} :answer-field/id #uuid "2875f717-ca5d-504c-966a-a3608a5a07bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186107211}] :question/id #uuid "2755d3ad-f502-5322-8d2b-3addfcafe3f4" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-124750" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1453/Source/Images/q-124750.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{5}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sec θ$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111269 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cdae874-74c2-5cdc-ade9-9d42f95435d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109710} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1d41af-b924-5aac-a84d-8c035820721d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109711} {:answer/id #uuid "defe7f3d-b6f8-5990-8c98-819c312f60cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109712} {:answer/id #uuid "07648329-6039-5609-860e-c78881ef81fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109713} {:answer/id #uuid "4a4e9d83-f971-55cc-b350-a5387323290a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad1d41af-b924-5aac-a84d-8c035820721d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109711} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a2a25d-67a7-5b6d-8a3a-8e7117aac155" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109709}] :question/id #uuid "0efeeff3-47fe-5165-905e-7ef041b1d387" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-65073" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1453/Source/Images/q-65073.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\tan θ$."}]}
